Starter PIDoc source for Astro Color Mixer v0.9.3-beta. Formal PixInsight documentation integration to be validated.

\title Astro Color Mixer FAQ and Practical Guide

\section What Is Astro Color Mixer?

Astro Color Mixer is a nonlinear RGB color and luminance refinement tool for astrophotography. It is intended for images that have already been calibrated, integrated, color balanced, and stretched.

\section Workflow Position

Use the tool after calibration, integration, background correction, color calibration, and nonlinear stretch. It is a post-stretch finishing tool rather than a linear calibration tool.

\section Image Type

Stars Present is the safer mode for images that still contain normal stars, bright stellar cores, and halos. Starless is intended for images where stars have been removed, allowing more freedom in nebulae, galaxies, dust, and faint structures.

This setting does not remove stars and does not create a star mask.

\section Basic Workflow

1. Open a nonlinear RGB image.
2. Choose Image Type.
3. Start with Base Pass.
4. Make broad H/S/L changes.
5. Use probe, histogram, and polar plot.
6. Refine Width and Feather.
7. On starless data, use Selected Band Blur only when a hard selected-band mask edge is visible.
8. Use Range Mask for luminance targeting.
9. Add targeted Refinement Passes.
10. Inspect preview and mask views.
11. Apply to New Image and save an Adjustment Set if needed.

\section Color Bands

- Red / H-alpha
- Orange / Dust and Galaxy Cores
- Yellow / Warm Stars
- Green / Cast Control
- Cyan / OIII
- Blue / Reflection Nebula
- Purple / Violet Cleanup
- Magenta / Halo Cleanup

\section Core Concepts

Hue rotates color direction. Saturation scales color intensity. Luminance brightens or darkens the active band. Width controls hue span. Feather controls transition softness. Range Mask limits the active pass by luminance. Neutral / Low-Saturation handles weak-color pixels where hue is unreliable.

\section Selected Band Blur

Selected Band Blur is a spatial blur control for the active selected-band mask. It is different from Feather: Feather softens the transition across hue distance, while Blur spatially smooths the final mask across neighboring image pixels.

Blur is only active in Starless mode. In Stars Present mode it is disabled because spatially blurring a color mask can bleed adjustments into star cores, halos, and adjacent stellar structures. Use it for starless color-mask edge cleanup, starting with 1 px and increasing only as needed up to 5 px.

\section Low-Saturation Galaxies

Some broadband galaxy images have real but very weak color in outer arms, dust, and faint halo structures. Protect Low Sat may reduce slider response in these pixels because low saturation makes hue less reliable.

Turning off Protect Low Sat can help reveal faint blue, cyan, orange, or red galaxy color, but it also raises the risk of blotchy color, noisy hue patches, and colored star halos. The safest workflow is to use a starless image, inspect the Band Mask and Combined Mask, and build the result with multiple smaller passes rather than one extreme pass.

\section Refinement Passes

Refinement Passes are sequential ordered passes. Base Pass handles broad work, and later passes handle targeted cleanup. They are not Photoshop layers and do not provide blend modes or opacity.

\section Diagnostics

The probe reports luminance, hue, and saturation from preview pixels. The histogram summarizes preview luminance and helps place Range Mask. The polar plot summarizes hue and saturation distribution. Mask views show selected band, Range Mask, or combined influence. In Starless mode, Current Band Mask and Combined Mask reflect active Selected Band Blur.

\section Adjustment Sets

Adjustment Sets are JSON files that preserve passes, band values, Width, Feather, selected-band Blur values, Range Mask settings, neutral luminance settings, image type, and related working state.
